#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124302 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/id #uuid "4b1227cb-7cf9-534d-a22f-e7acf7df9dc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4830" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ![](images/e9/e98eb00ac7ee6ffb83d4a902d92e9bda4434e6bfaeae1fb91a210849d3e1991f.svg) is a linearly independent set in $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\nII. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ![](images/71/716877f60112610eae19b094ca691edf0c0d2ecdffc54dc0b785d66c96e64909.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/cc/ccd021603e72ceb60be92eabec243bfdd5ce33eb22a6b801030a05efc02a861d.svg) is a basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n![](images/a1/a16ee652347a67e66994875711dff835418bfcfb4f85a058a80f9164af525cab.svg)\nis true if and only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n![](images/f1/f173928232bbfd7680c2952250c78e7ba354bd4851cb4783bd531cc683044db5.svg)\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n![](images/74/7449707a6aa218f65cc22db3a56e8ed9391e3b5e562301e77fd62cb2f3dad20a.svg)\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry $a_{11} = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/b9/b952b253393b4d14572b41ad1ca265e3b858edeec36e4c937f644621ae497294.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124303 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "91989c25-1d0d-5639-b3e1-7f7479322b7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4831" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\nII. $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}$ spans $V$\n\nIII. $\\left\\{  -\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+{x}_{2}(-{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}({b}_{3}+{b}_{1}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n![](images/62/625e812c25818cec68c6a5bcd59a39945feb09526607244e42059ed8af6f9b08.svg)\nAlso, $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+x_3\\mathbf{b}_3\n=\nx_2\\mathbf{b}_2+(x_3-x_1)(-\\mathbf{b}_1)+x_3 (\\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1})\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In order for $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}$ to span $V,$ every element of $\\mathcal{B}$ must be able to be expressed as a linear combination of elements of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}.$ But the vector $\\mathbf{b}_1 \\in \\mathcal{B}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the elements of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If $\\left\\{  -\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ not both zero such that\n\n$$\nx_1(-\\mathbf{b_2}) + x_2(3\\mathbf{b_1}) = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThen, we would have\n\n$$\n(3x_2) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_1} + (-x_1) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_2} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_3= \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nBut this would mean that $\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent, which contradicts the fact that $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis of $V.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124403 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] :question/id #uuid "92adb651-e2db-5296-817d-aea249afe2b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8269" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\big\\{ 0,t^2 \\big\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$\n\nII. $-5t^2$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$\n\nIII. $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-5t^2 = 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + (-5) \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a10dfc-afa4-52d6-9bf7-b580f906a755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447032} {:answer/id #uuid "299a0ce7-32c9-5434-88c4-7d63337cd0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447034} {:answer/id #uuid "6e05ed0b-0f3c-5c51-9fcd-1cb42a9bc2e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447041} {:answer/id #uuid "84245521-5afe-5940-8979-375abcac5604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447043} {:answer/id #uuid "e75c2b7e-4724-50f4-baef-5d83a5a25436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e75c2b7e-4724-50f4-baef-5d83a5a25436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447050} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e1e424-505b-5097-b514-ba9bc2cc90d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a024553e-c27e-5c68-a828-a6ec8223e683" :question/math-academy-id "q-107130" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nII. $2+{t}^{2}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot 2+{x}_{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n2+{t}^{2}=1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot {t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the polynomial $t$ can't be written as a linear combination of\n\n$$\n\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these combinations result in polynomials without terms of the form $at.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013e97ca-b56e-5aee-9c9f-d304a8a1dbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447031} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5613e5-d85e-5aa4-87d6-6ad009986011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447033} {:answer/id #uuid "384b6eb0-e2bf-5f86-af7f-3398204e70e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447036} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb0c223-1e6e-53a8-9673-4bcd81bc316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447038} {:answer/id #uuid "c794a74a-633c-5598-9aae-0206903fc293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "013e97ca-b56e-5aee-9c9f-d304a8a1dbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447031} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2baa79b-54b3-5737-acdd-b3964add504b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2dd6c5c5-53ed-575f-8c9f-dc822f51b9e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-107537" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nIII. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}(2{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(2{x}_{2}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}{b}_{2}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The set $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the third vector is a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that the set is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2fd8bc6-c3b4-5fff-b149-a38924d6eb90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122397} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5f5063-5416-5eba-a306-fc0f429c1a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122398} {:answer/id #uuid "d4af1cdd-c99f-5185-869c-921c2c5b872b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122399} {:answer/id #uuid "e237732d-bbfd-50f6-ba1c-009e6f76f816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122400} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2288a7-a795-5272-8a81-7ddae79bf223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122401}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2afa0b50-032e-5db8-81c4-083febcf78df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122396}] :question/id #uuid "96532d4b-53ae-5b0b-a44e-d0ba6b721786" :question/math-academy-id "q-36308" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $R_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. ${0, t, t^{2}}$ is a linearly independent set in $R_{2}[t]$\n2. $t^{2} - 2t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0, t, t^{2}}$\n3. ${0, t, t^{2}}$ is a basis of $R_{2}[t]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4374878-9079-5ae8-b4fb-c94da60a5b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122403} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a87a06-94fe-5192-9f51-894660a8a27e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122404} {:answer/id #uuid "196b08d2-a00b-51da-93c2-49746e140cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122405} {:answer/id #uuid "199df1de-8548-5048-b04c-6b828a95aeb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122406} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4cf1d8-593b-5fe3-9148-dc675c4f85df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122407}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4dbc5957-4c70-590d-bd74-bdc5647526ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122402}] :question/id #uuid "24ec3a38-129c-5be9-91d3-dbe8f97d4c69" :question/math-academy-id "q-36309" :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ be a basis of a vector space $V$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. ${b_{1}, b_{2}}$ is linearly independent\n2. ${b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}, w}$ is linearly independent\n3. ${2b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ is a basis of $V$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf3f30e4-0f89-5940-99e4-dcf3910c0fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122613} {:answer/id #uuid "d5288fa3-1db8-5e79-92c4-7e99c244d05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122614} {:answer/id #uuid "24437291-99fb-588b-8dab-62cb55817f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122615} {:answer/id #uuid "2a686b7e-36e0-5679-9b2e-2caae66b890c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122616} {:answer/id #uuid "61daf739-1f7c-576c-9452-8ad79a827c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122617}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8e8895a9-c9e9-5618-ac22-04862d99cf8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122612}] :question/id #uuid "03a15270-f806-567d-89e2-38996d20cbe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-36571" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $M_{2}(R)$ of all $2 \\cdot 2$ matrices over $R$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a linearly independent set in $M_{2}(R)$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n3. ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a basis of $M_{2}(R)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bbdee2f-94bd-572f-91ae-a989e989cefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454060} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6016de-8322-5611-abac-11c53498b5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bbdee2f-94bd-572f-91ae-a989e989cefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454060} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b86c8c7-ce4a-5843-b124-fd92dc984de2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5061063-49ba-56ef-8537-f424f6dc8a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454062} {:answer/id #uuid "710153ca-da8e-5423-9cbc-1f6713a46398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "710153ca-da8e-5423-9cbc-1f6713a46398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454063} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7647c92-3c6a-55d5-b2a1-537c5b726446" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0743a148-5538-58b8-be74-ca107d1c906d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454064} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c81ac2-78b7-59ca-8a48-01ff07cdd26a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0743a148-5538-58b8-be74-ca107d1c906d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454064} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccfdb226-5087-511e-8a84-93a1b34f530a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3557614-de80-516a-bb07-7b50ae99a56a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80631" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ are bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- **II.** ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- **III.** ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-2{b}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}(5{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2{x}_{1}){b}_{3}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+(5{x}_{3}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}(3{b}_{1})+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}(5{b}_{2})+(-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2})(-2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1429c0a-89af-5805-8580-b73e7cd5d01a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452137} {:answer/id #uuid "624e0c32-5d2c-56d6-81a3-b8d4282b2eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1429c0a-89af-5805-8580-b73e7cd5d01a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452137} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e7b7fcb-d746-5098-8534-b43bcfff8673" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452916} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e3f86b7-428b-5f3c-96de-c85c2894488b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452139} {:answer/id #uuid "52cc4177-3ee9-5140-bd61-3db7f9ff2e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52cc4177-3ee9-5140-bd61-3db7f9ff2e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452140} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9c6abba-e799-5070-8c49-022c9a387a3f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452917} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6e785fe-8b18-56e6-9291-7b0b3e83aeef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452141} {:answer/id #uuid "b145c6d5-7d89-5fe4-b6c1-fa9b85036ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6e785fe-8b18-56e6-9291-7b0b3e83aeef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452141} :answer-field/id #uuid "4105ae7e-75bf-54ba-80a7-8977f2a90571" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf9e33e0-00b4-5d7d-a1a7-55573563c2a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-94850" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\n- **II.** $-{t}^{3}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$\n\n- **III.** $\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-{t}^{3}=0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b2c106c-830c-550f-9834-4e30ffa6398f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485925} {:answer/id #uuid "70454c64-8842-5c74-90d5-a65a027e02d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485957} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5a2ff6-779a-5eee-95b7-e7c15c73d9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486043} {:answer/id #uuid "a2106cd2-d4c4-5485-bd50-b22dc45d0703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486052} {:answer/id #uuid "d51926b0-1f7a-5517-b205-7d50c8ea1fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b2c106c-830c-550f-9834-4e30ffa6398f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485925} :answer-field/id #uuid "20236f24-a672-571d-8a2d-4eb8e53f4a42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e6a690a-1af9-54cd-96d8-6699f35c43cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-94865" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that all linear combinations of \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nresult in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c860248f-2167-58c8-87fa-5053ba205a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452167} {:answer/id #uuid "28fe9ba6-8189-5224-bf66-47ad63b7bfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28fe9ba6-8189-5224-bf66-47ad63b7bfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452168} :answer-field/id #uuid "06c3b965-56bb-5af4-b2ab-93b39a35d64b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452931} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88afd962-6b15-5245-88e9-243e062057b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452169} {:answer/id #uuid "c4197e6b-54ce-5ade-90d9-6328e5f4cd64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4197e6b-54ce-5ade-90d9-6328e5f4cd64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452170} :answer-field/id #uuid "59b54ffc-e9ac-58a8-b1a8-a4ab9361e15c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db6df491-5fe1-5fe6-a1b3-064e0e51ac82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452171} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c17006-ca48-5392-8bef-d97644e75c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5c17006-ca48-5392-8bef-d97644e75c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452172} :answer-field/id #uuid "94a15b92-9c3a-50c1-9d95-1e6a32a695cc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e6efc92-73ea-52ca-ba80-456c38800684" :question/math-academy-id "q-94870" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\n- **II.** $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\n- **III.** $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}=a\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+d\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
